-		<English>[H1]Happiness[\H1][PARAGRAPH:2]If he wants to be successful in Civilization IV, even the most ruthless dictator must give some thought to his people's happiness. A happy citizen is a productive citizen. An unhappy citizen is a useless drag on his [LINK=CONCEPT_CITIES]City[\LINK], producing nothing but still eating as much as anyone else.[BOLD]Caveat (AdvCiv):[\BOLD]Part of this page may well be outdated. Too much work to keep a list of all sources of happiness and anger updated. [PARAGRAPH:2][H2]Effects of Unhappiness[\H2][PARAGRAPH:2]Each citizen in a city has a job to do. Many are employed working the land around the city, and some few may be [LINK=CONCEPT_SPECIALISTS]Specialists[\LINK], performing various other critical work. When a citizen is "unhappy" he or she refuses to work, thus reducing the city's laborers or specialists by one. This slacker still consumes two food each turn, however, and a city with too many unhappy citizens faces loss of productivity, commerce, a slowdown of the generation of great people, and perhaps [LINK=CONCEPT_FOOD]Starvation[\LINK]. [PARAGRAPH:2]Rather than allowing a city to grow until it is unhappy, it is often a good idea to use specialists or the "Avoid Growth" governor command to halt a city at the size just before it would become unhappy. When more happiness is added later, the city can then go back to growing again.[PARAGRAPH:2][H2]Determining Happiness[\H2][PARAGRAPH:2]Each city has its own individual happiness level. There are many factors which can cause unhappiness in a city, with overpopulation and war being two of the most common (see below for a full list). Each source of unhappiness generates one or more "unhappy faces" (see [LINK=CONCEPT_CITIES]Cities[\LINK]). There are also many factors which produce happiness in a city, with religion and access to certain resources being two of the most important. Each of these generates one or more "happy faces."[PARAGRAPH:2]To determine a city's happiness, compare the number of happy faces to unhappy faces. If a city has as many or more happy faces as it has unhappy ones, all of the citizens in that city are happy. If the city has more unhappy faces than happy ones, a number of citizens equal to the difference will be unhappy. (Thus, if a city has five happy faces and seven unhappy faces, two of the city's citizens will refuse to work.)[PARAGRAPH:2]If a city has at least one unhappy citizen, an unhappy face will appear next to the city's name on the main map.[PARAGRAPH:2][H2]Causes of Unhappiness[\H2][PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Overpopulation:[\BOLD] Every citizen in a city generates one unhappy face. The bigger the population, the more unhappy everybody is.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]War Weariness:[\BOLD] As a war continues, more and more citizens become unhappy about it. This is especially true in cities that you have recently captured from the civilization you're fighting.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Cultural Inferiority:[\BOLD] A city will become unhappy if it is being "culturally dominated" by a nearby foreign city (see [LINK=CONCEPT_CULTURE]Culture[\LINK] and [LINK=CONCEPT_BORDERS]Borders[\LINK]).[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Undefended:[\BOLD] A city becomes unhappy if you have no military units in that city. The type of unit does not matter, so make sure to have at least one unit in every city.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Slavery and the Draft:[\BOLD] Citizens are unhappy if you work them to death (see the [LINK=CIVIC_SLAVERY]Slavery[\LINK] civic). They don't much like it either if you forcibly draft them into the military (see the [LINK=CIVIC_NATIONHOOD]Nationhood[\LINK] civic).[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Emancipation:[\BOLD] Citizens become unhappy if other civilizations have adopted the [LINK=CIVIC_EMANCIPATION]Emancipation[\LINK] civic and you have not. The more civilizations that have adopted emancipation, the more unhappy become the citizens of the remaining holdouts.[PARAGRAPH:2][H2]Causes of Happiness[\H2][PARAGRAPH:2]There are plenty of them. Here are a few:[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Resources:[\BOLD] Many [LINK=CONCEPT_RESOURCES]Resources[\LINK] produce happiness: [LINK=BONUS_FUR]Fur[\LINK] and [LINK=BONUS_GOLD]Gold[\LINK], to name two.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Buildings:[\BOLD] Temples, [LINK=BUILDING_COLOSSEUM]Colosseums[\LINK], and [LINK=BUILDING_THEATRE]Theatres[\LINK] are some early buildings which produce happiness. The [LINK=BUILDING_JAIL]Jail[\LINK] removes some unhappiness caused by war weariness.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Wonders:[\BOLD] The [LINK=BUILDING_GLOBE_THEATRE]Globe Theatre[\LINK] removes all unhappiness in the city where it is built. [LINK=BUILDING_ROCKNROLL]Rock-n-Roll[\LINK] produces the Hit Singles resource, which generates one happiness.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Civics:[\BOLD] The [LINK=CIVIC_POLICE_STATE]Police State[\LINK] civic reduces war weariness. [LINK=CIVIC_FREE_SPEECH]Free Speech[\LINK] produces happiness (in combination with [LINK=BUILDING_BROADCAST_TOWER]Broadcast Towers[\LINK]).[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Culture Button:[\BOLD] After discovering the [LINK=TECH_DRAMA]Drama[\LINK] technology, you can devote a percentage of your civilization's commerce to the [LINK=CONCEPT_CULTURE]Culture Button[\LINK], which increases both culture and happiness. Certain buildings like [LINK=BUILDING_THEATRE]Theatres[\LINK] and [LINK=BUILDING_COLOSSEUM]Colosseums[\LINK] increase the happiness effect from the Culture Button even further.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]And More:[\BOLD] As you play, you will discover many other factors that can increase or decrease a city's happiness.[PARAGRAPH:2]</English>
+		<English>[H1]Happiness[\H1][PARAGRAPH:2]If he wants to be successful in Civilization IV, even the most ruthless dictator must give some thought to his people's happiness. A happy citizen is a productive citizen. An unhappy citizen is a useless drag on his [LINK=CONCEPT_CITIES]City[\LINK], producing nothing but still eating as much as anyone else.[BOLD]Caveat (AdvCiv):[\BOLD]Part of this page may well be outdated. Too much work to keep a list of all sources of happiness and anger updated. [PARAGRAPH:2][H2]Effects of Unhappiness[\H2][PARAGRAPH:2]Each citizen in a city has a job to do. Many are employed working the land around the city, and some few may be [LINK=CONCEPT_SPECIALISTS]Specialists[\LINK], performing various other critical work. When a citizen is "unhappy" he or she refuses to work, thus reducing the city's laborers or specialists by one. This slacker still consumes two food each turn, however, and a city with too many unhappy citizens faces loss of productivity, commerce, a slowdown of the generation of great people, and perhaps [LINK=CONCEPT_FOOD]Starvation[\LINK]. [PARAGRAPH:2]Rather than allowing a city to grow until it is unhappy, it is often a good idea to use specialists or the "Avoid Growth" governor command to halt a city at the size just before it would become unhappy. When more happiness is added later, the city can then go back to growing again.[PARAGRAPH:2][H2]Determining Happiness[\H2][PARAGRAPH:2]Each city has its own individual happiness level. There are many factors which can cause unhappiness in a city, with overpopulation and war being two of the most common (see below for a full list). Each source of unhappiness generates one or more "unhappy faces" (see [LINK=CONCEPT_CITIES]Cities[\LINK]). There are also many factors which produce happiness in a city, with religion and access to certain resources being two of the most important. Each of these generates one or more "happy faces."[PARAGRAPH:2]To determine a city's happiness, compare the number of happy faces to unhappy faces. If a city has as many or more happy faces as it has unhappy ones, all of the citizens in that city are happy. If the city has more unhappy faces than happy ones, a number of citizens equal to the difference will be unhappy. (Thus, if a city has five happy faces and seven unhappy faces, two of the city's citizens will refuse to work.)[PARAGRAPH:2]If a city has at least one unhappy citizen, an unhappy face will appear next to the city's name on the main map.[PARAGRAPH:2][H2]Causes of Unhappiness[\H2][PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Overpopulation:[\BOLD] Every citizen in a city generates one unhappy face. The bigger the population, the more unhappy everybody is.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]War Weariness:[\BOLD] As a war continues, more and more citizens become unhappy about it. This is especially true in cities that you have recently captured from the civilization you're fighting.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Cultural Inferiority:[\BOLD] A city will become unhappy if it is being "culturally dominated" by a nearby foreign city (see [LINK=CONCEPT_CULTURE]Culture[\LINK] and [LINK=CONCEPT_BORDERS]Borders[\LINK]).[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Undefended:[\BOLD] A city becomes unhappy if you have no military units in that city. The type of unit does not matter, so make sure to have at least one unit in every city. [BOLD]AdvCiv[\BOLD] removes this source of unhappiness upon reaching the technology [LINK=TECH_NATIONALISM]Nationalism[\LINK].[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Slavery and the Draft:[\BOLD] Citizens are unhappy if you work them to death (see the [LINK=CIVIC_SLAVERY]Slavery[\LINK] civic). They don't much like it either if you forcibly draft them into the military (see the [LINK=CIVIC_NATIONHOOD]Nationhood[\LINK] civic).[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Emancipation:[\BOLD] Citizens become unhappy if other civilizations have adopted the [LINK=CIVIC_EMANCIPATION]Emancipation[\LINK] civic and you have not. The more civilizations that have adopted emancipation, the more unhappy become the citizens of the remaining holdouts.[PARAGRAPH:2][H2]Causes of Happiness[\H2][PARAGRAPH:2]There are plenty of them. Here are a few:[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Resources:[\BOLD] Many [LINK=CONCEPT_RESOURCES]Resources[\LINK] produce happiness: [LINK=BONUS_FUR]Fur[\LINK] and [LINK=BONUS_GOLD]Gold[\LINK], to name two.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Buildings:[\BOLD] Temples, [LINK=BUILDING_COLOSSEUM]Colosseums[\LINK], and [LINK=BUILDING_THEATRE]Theatres[\LINK] are some early buildings which produce happiness. The [LINK=BUILDING_JAIL]Jail[\LINK] removes some unhappiness caused by war weariness.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Wonders:[\BOLD] The [LINK=BUILDING_GLOBE_THEATRE]Globe Theatre[\LINK] removes all unhappiness in the city where it is built. [LINK=BUILDING_ROCKNROLL]Rock-n-Roll[\LINK] produces the Hit Singles resource, which generates one happiness.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Civics:[\BOLD] The [LINK=CIVIC_POLICE_STATE]Police State[\LINK] civic reduces war weariness. [LINK=CIVIC_FREE_SPEECH]Free Speech[\LINK] produces happiness (in combination with [LINK=BUILDING_BROADCAST_TOWER]Broadcast Towers[\LINK]).[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Culture Button:[\BOLD] After discovering the [LINK=TECH_DRAMA]Drama[\LINK] technology, you can devote a percentage of your civilization's commerce to the [LINK=CONCEPT_CULTURE]Culture Button[\LINK], which increases both culture and happiness. Certain buildings like [LINK=BUILDING_THEATRE]Theatres[\LINK] and [LINK=BUILDING_COLOSSEUM]Colosseums[\LINK] increase the happiness effect from the Culture Button even further.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]And More:[\BOLD] As you play, you will discover many other factors that can increase or decrease a city's happiness.[PARAGRAPH:2]</English>
 		<French>[H1]Bonheur[\H1][PARAGRAPH:2]Pour gagner &#224; Civilization IV, m&#234;me le dictateur le plus impitoyable doit se soucier quelque peu du bonheur de ses sujets. Un individu heureux est plus productif. Un individu malheureux est un poids mort pour sa [LINK=CONCEPT_CITIES]ville[\LINK], il ne produit rien mais mange autant que les autres.[PARAGRAPH:2][H2]Effets du malheur[\H2][PARAGRAPH:2]Chaque habitant d'une ville a un travail &#224; accomplir. La plupart travaillent la terre environnante, et certains sont des [LINK=CONCEPT_SPECIALISTS]sp&#233;cialistes[\LINK] charg&#233;s d'accomplir des t&#226;ches importantes. Lorsqu'un habitant est "m&#233;content", il ou elle refuse de travailler, ce qui diminue d'un le nombre des ouvriers ou des sp&#233;cialistes. Ce fain&#233;ant continue n&#233;anmoins de consommer deux unit&#233;s de nourritures par tour. Ainsi, une ville qui abrite trop de m&#233;contents perd en productivit&#233; et en commerce, voit ralentir la cr&#233;ation de personnages illustres et risque m&#234;me la [LINK=CONCEPT_FOOD]famine[\LINK]. [PARAGRAPH:2]Plut&#244;t que de laisser une ville g&#233;n&#233;rer des m&#233;contents, il est souvent pr&#233;f&#233;rable d'utiliser des sp&#233;cialistes ou d'ordonner de "bloquer la croissance" juste avant que la population de la ville n'atteigne la taille critique. Plus tard, quand le bonheur aura &#233;t&#233; am&#233;lior&#233;, il sera possible de relancer la croissance de la ville.[PARAGRAPH:2][H2]D&#233;terminer le bonheur[\H2][PARAGRAPH:2]Chaque ville a son propre niveau de bonheur. Plusieurs facteurs peuvent g&#233;n&#233;rer du malheur dans les villes, les plus communs &#233;tant la surpopulation et la guerre (voir plus bas la liste compl&#232;te). Chaque source de malheur g&#233;n&#232;re au moins un "visage m&#233;content" (voir [LINK=CONCEPT_CITIES]villes[\LINK]). Plusieurs facteurs g&#233;n&#232;rent &#233;galement du bonheur, deux des plus importants &#233;tant la religion et l'acc&#232;s &#224; certaines ressources. Chacun de ces facteurs g&#233;n&#232;re au moins un "visage heureux".[PARAGRAPH:2]Pour d&#233;terminer le bonheur d'une ville, comparez le nombre de visages heureux &#224; celui des m&#233;contents. Si une ville a autant ou plus de visages heureux que de m&#233;contents, toute la population est heureuse. Si le nombre des visages m&#233;contents est sup&#233;rieur &#224; celui des heureux, un nombre d'habitants &#233;gal &#224; la diff&#233;rence sera m&#233;content (dans une ville qui a 5 visages heureux et 7 m&#233;contents, deux habitants refuseront de travailler).[PARAGRAPH:2]Si une ville compte au moins un habitant m&#233;content, un visage m&#233;content appara&#238;t &#224; c&#244;t&#233; de son nom sur la carte principale.[PARAGRAPH:2][H2]Causes de m&#233;contentement[\H2][PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Surpopulation :[\BOLD] chaque habitant de la ville g&#233;n&#232;re un visage m&#233;content. Plus la population est nombreuse, plus elle est m&#233;contente.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Lassitude de la guerre :[\BOLD] plus une guerre dure, plus les habitants sont m&#233;contents. C'est particuli&#232;rement vrai pour les villes que vous avez r&#233;cemment prises &#224; votre adversaire actuel.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Inf&#233;riorit&#233; culturelle :[\BOLD] une ville "culturellement domin&#233;e" par une cit&#233; &#233;trang&#232;re devient m&#233;contente (voir [LINK=CONCEPT_CULTURE]culture[\LINK] et [LINK=CONCEPT_BORDERS]fronti&#232;res[\LINK]).[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Non d&#233;fendue :[\BOLD] une ville qui n'abrite pas d'unit&#233;s militaires devient m&#233;contente. Peu importe le type d'unit&#233;, veillez &#224; conserver au moins une unit&#233; militaire dans chaque ville.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Esclavage et conscription :[\BOLD] des habitants que vous exploitez au maximum deviennent m&#233;contents (voir le r&#233;gime de l'[LINK=CIVIC_SLAVERY]esclavage[\LINK]). Ils n'appr&#233;cient pas non plus d'&#234;tre enr&#244;l&#233;s de force dans l'arm&#233;e (voir le r&#233;gime de la [LINK=CIVIC_NATIONHOOD]nation[\LINK]).[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Emancipation :[\BOLD] si d'autres civilisations choisissent le r&#233;gime de [LINK=CIVIC_EMANCIPATION]l'&#233;mancipation[\LINK] et pas vous, la population devient m&#233;contente. Plus il y a de civilisations concern&#233;es, plus la population sera m&#233;contente.[PARAGRAPH:2][H2]Causes de bonheur[\H2][PARAGRAPH:2]Elles sont nombreuses, mais en voici quelques-unes :[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Ressources :[\BOLD] de nombreuses [LINK=CONCEPT_RESOURCES]ressources[\LINK] g&#233;n&#232;rent du bonheur, comme par exemple la [LINK=BONUS_FUR]fourrure[\LINK] et l'[LINK=BONUS_GOLD]or[\LINK].[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]B&#226;timents : [\BOLD] petit lieu de culte, [LINK=BUILDING_COLOSSEUM]colis&#233;e[\LINK] et [LINK=BUILDING_THEATRE]th&#233;&#226;tre[\LINK] sont quelques-uns des b&#226;timents qui g&#233;n&#232;rent du bonheur. La [LINK=BUILDING_JAIL]prison[\LINK] &#233;limine une partie du m&#233;contentement cr&#233;&#233; par la lassitude de la guerre.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Merveilles :[\BOLD] le [LINK=BUILDING_GLOBE_THEATRE]Th&#233;&#226;tre du Globe[\LINK] &#233;limine tout le m&#233;contentement dans la ville o&#249; il est construit. Le [LINK=BUILDING_ROCKNROLL]Rock'n'Roll[\LINK] produit la ressource Tubes, qui g&#233;n&#232;re du bonheur.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]R&#233;gimes :[\BOLD] le r&#233;gime de l'[LINK=CIVIC_POLICE_STATE]Etat policier[\LINK] supprime la lassitude de la guerre. La [LINK=CIVIC_FREE_SPEECH]Libert&#233; d'expression[\LINK] produit du bonheur (en combinaison avec les [LINK=BUILDING_BROADCAST_TOWER]Tours de radiodiffusion[\LINK]).[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Bouton culture :[\BOLD] apr&#232;s avoir d&#233;couvert la technologie [LINK=DRAMA]th&#233;&#226;tre[\LINK], vous pouvez consacrer une partie du commerce de votre civilisation au [LINK=CONCEPT_CULTURE]bouton culture[\LINK], ce qui augmente &#224; la fois la culture et le bonheur. Certains b&#226;timents, comme les [LINK=BUILDING_THEATRE]th&#233;&#226;tres[\LINK] et les [LINK=BUILDING_COLOSSEUM]colis&#233;es[\LINK] augmentent l'effet du bouton culture.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Et plus :[\BOLD] au fil du jeu, vous d&#233;couvrirez d'autres facteurs pouvant augmenter ou r&#233;duire le bonheur d'une ville.[PARAGRAPH:2]</French>
-		<German>[H1]Zufriedenheit[\H1][PARAGRAPH:2]Auch der r&#252;cksichtsloseste Diktator muss die Zufriedenheit seines Volkes im Auge behalten, wenn er in Civilization IV Erfolg haben will. Zufriedene B&#252;rger sind produktiv. Unzufriedene B&#252;rger schaden einer [LINK=CONCEPT_CITIES]Stadt[\LINK] eher, da sie nichts produzieren, aber trotzdem versorgt werden m&#252;ssen.[PARAGRAPH:2][H2]Folgen der Unzufriedenheit[\H2][PARAGRAPH:2]Jeder Bewohner einer Stadt hat eine Aufgabe. Einige bestellen das Land, andere sind m&#246;glicherweise [LINK=CONCEPT_SPECIALISTS]Spezialisten[\LINK] mit anderen wichtigen Aufgaben. Ist ein B&#252;rger unzufrieden, verweigert er die Arbeit, verbraucht aber weiterhin 2 Nahrungseinheiten pro Runde. Leben in einer Stadt zu viele unzufriedene B&#252;rger, sinken die Produktivit&#228;t und die Wirtschaftsertr&#228;ge einer Stadt und die Entwicklung Gro&#223;er Pers&#246;nlichkeiten ger&#228;t ins Stocken. Im Extremfall droht sogar eine [LINK=CONCEPT_FOOD]Hungersnot[\LINK].[PARAGRAPH:2]Lassen Sie eine Stadt also nicht wachsen, bis ihre Bewohner unzufrieden sind, sondern bremsen Sie das Wachstum einer Stadt mit Spezialisten oder dem Wachstum vermeiden-Button. Ist die Bev&#246;lkerung wieder zufriedener, k&#246;nnen Sie das Wachstum der Stadt wieder beschleunigen.[PARAGRAPH:2][H2]Analyse der Zufriedenheit[\H2][PARAGRAPH:2]Jeder B&#252;rger hat eine Zufriedenheitsstufe, die von verschiedenen Faktoren wie &#220;berbev&#246;lkerung und Krieg beeinflusst wird. (Eine vollst&#228;ndige Liste finden Sie weiter unten). Unzufriedenheitsfaktoren werden durch "ungl&#252;ckliche Gesichter" angezeigt (siehe [LINK=CONCEPT_CITIES]St&#228;dte[\LINK]). Nat&#252;rlich gibt auch Faktoren, wie Religionen und bestimmte Ressourcen, die die Zufriedenheit der Menschen erh&#246;hen. Diese erkennen Sie an den angezeigten "gl&#252;cklichen Gesichtern".[PARAGRAPH:2]Wollen Sie die Zufriedenheit einer Stadt analysieren, m&#252;ssen Sie die Zahl der ungl&#252;cklichen und gl&#252;cklichen Gesichter vergleichen. Ist die Zahl der gl&#252;cklichen Gesichter gleich oder h&#246;her, sind alle B&#252;rger der Stadt zufrieden. Gibt es mehr ungl&#252;ckliche Gesichter, sind entsprechend viele Bewohner unzufrieden. Beispiel: In einer Stadt mit f&#252;nf gl&#252;ckliche und sieben ungl&#252;gliche Gesichtern sind zwei B&#252;rger unzufrieden und verweigern die Arbeit.[PARAGRAPH:2]Ist mindestens ein Bewohner einer Stadt unzufrieden, erscheint neben dem Namen der Stadt auf der Karte ein ungl&#252;ckliches Gesicht. [BOLD]Warnung (AdvCiv):[\BOLD]Teile dieses Artikels sind veraltet. Die Listen mit den Ursachen f�r Zufriedenheit und Unzufriedenheit aktuell zu halten, ist leider zu aufwendig.[PARAGRAPH:2][H2]Ursachen der Unzufriedenheit[\H2][PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]&#220;berbev&#246;lkerung[\BOLD]: Jeder B&#252;rger einer Stadt wird durch ein gl&#252;ckliches oder ungl&#252;ckliches Gesicht dargestellt. Je gr&#246;&#223;er die Bev&#246;lkerung ist, desto unzufriedener sind die Bewohner einer Stadt.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Kriegsm&#252;digkeit[\BOLD]: Im Laufe eines Krieges steigt die Unzufriedenheit der Menschen. Dies gilt vor allem f&#252;r erst k&#252;rzlich eroberte St&#228;dte.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Kulturelle Unterlegenheit:[\BOLD] Die Bewohner einer Stadt sind unzufrieden, wenn sie von einer anderen Stadt kulturell dominiert werden. Weitere Informationen dazu finden Sie in den Abschnitten [LINK=CONCEPT_CULTURE]Kultur[\LINK] und [LINK=CONCEPT_BORDERS]Grenzen[\LINK]). [BOLD]Fehlender Schutz:[\BOLD] Die Bewohner einer Stadt sind unzufrieden, wenn Sie in ihrer Stadt keine Soldaten stationiert haben.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Sklaverei und Rekrutierung[\BOLD]: Die Bewohner einer Stadt sind unzufrieden, wenn sie schuften m&#252;ssen. Weitere Informationen dazu finden Sie im Abschnitt [LINK=CIVIC_SLAVERY]Sklaverei[\LINK]). Wenn Sie die Bewohner einer Stadt zwangsrekrutieren, steigt ihre Unzufriedenheit ebenfalls. Weitere Informationen dazu finden Sie im Abschnitt [LINK=CIVIC_NATIONHOOD]Nationale Selbstst&#228;ndigkeit[\LINK]).[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Emanzipation:[\BOLD] Die Bewohner einer Stadt sind unzufrieden, wenn andere Zivilisationen die Staatsform [LINK=CIVIC_EMANCIPATION]Emanzipation[\LINK] vor Ihnen annehmen. Je mehr Zivilisationen diese Staatsform umsetzen, desto gr&#246;&#223;er ist die Unzufriedenheit der Bewohner aller anderen Zivilisationen.[PARAGRAPH:2][H2]Erh&#246;hung der Zufriedenheit[\H2][PARAGRAPH:2]Im Folgenden finden Sie einige M&#246;glichkeiten, die Zufriedenheit Ihrer Bev&#246;lkerung zu erh&#246;hen:[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Ressourcen:[\BOLD] Viele [LINK=CONCEPT_RESOURCES]Ressourcen[\LINK], wie [LINK=BONUS_FUR]Pelze[\LINK] und [LINK=BONUS_GOLD]Gold[\LINK] f&#246;rdern die Zufriedenheit. [PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Geb&#228;ude:[\BOLD] Tempel, [LINK=BUILDING_COLOSSEUM]Kolossen[\LINK] und [LINK=BUILDING_THEATRE]Theater[\LINK] f&#246;rdern die Zufriedenheit ebenfalls. [LINK=BUILDING_JAIL]Gef&#228;ngnisse[\LINK] verringern die durch Kriegsm&#252;digkeit ausgel&#246;ste Unzufriedenheit.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Wunder:[BOLD] Das [LINK=BUILDING_GLOBE_THEATRE]Globe Theatre[\LINK] beseitigt in der Stadt, in der es gebaut wird, jegliche Unzufriedenheit. Der [LINK=BUILDING_ROCKNROLL]Rock 'n Roll[\LINK] f&#246;rdert die Zufriedenheit der Menschen ebenfalls.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Staatsformen:[\BOLD] In einem [LINK=CIVIC_POLICE_STATE]Polizeistaat[\LINK] gibt es weniger Kriegsm&#252;digkeit. Die [LINK=CIVIC_FREE_SPEECH]Meinungsfreiheit[\LINK] f&#246;rdert die Zufriedenheit (in Kombination mit [LINK=BUILDING_BROADCAST_TOWER]Funkt&#252;rmen[\LINK].[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Kultur-Button:[\BOLD] Nach der Erforschung der Technologie [LINK=TECH_DRAMA]Drama[\LINK] k&#246;nnen Sie einen Teil Ihrer Wirtschaftsertr&#228;ge dem [LINK=CONCEPT_CULTURE]Kultur-Button[\LINK] zuweisen. Dieser f&#246;rdert die Kultur und die Zufriedenheit. Geb&#228;ude, wie [LINK=BUILDING_THEATRE]Theater[\LINK] und [LINK=BUILDING_COLOSSEUM]Kolossen[\LINK], steigern die Wirkung des Kultur-Buttons zus&#228;tzlich.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Hinweis:[\BOLD] Im Laufe des Spiels lernen Sie viele weitere M&#246;glichkeiten kennen, die Zufriedenheit einer Stadt positiv oder negativ zu beeinflussen.[PARAGRAPH:2]</German>
